          %h2#basic_idea Basic idea
          %p
            %strong Algebraic weak factorization systems
            (AWFS) are algebraizations of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} comonad
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrow+category"} arrow category
          %p
            Algebraic weak factorization systems were originally called
            %strong natural weak factorization systems
            by Grandis and Tholen.
          %h2#preliminaries Preliminaries
          %p
            Recall a
            %strong functorial factorization
            on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo :
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E : K
            %sup 2
            →
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %sup 3
            that is a section to the composition functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^1 : 2 \rightarrow 3
            between the ordinal categories.
          %p
            Explicitly,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            factors a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi f
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (u,v) : f \Rightarrow g
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi K
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^{2}
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi v
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                  %mover
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi E
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Lf
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Lg
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi E
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi u
                              %mo ,
                              %mi v
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Rf
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi Rg
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi v
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\cdot &amp;\stackrel{u}{\to}&amp; \cdot
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^f
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow^g
                  \\\
                  \\cdot &amp;\stackrel{v}{\to}&amp; \cdot
                  } \array{ &amp; &amp; } \stackrel{E}{\mapsto} \array{ &amp;&amp;}
                  \\array{
                  \\cdot &amp;\stackrel{u}{\to}&amp; \cdot
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{Lf}
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow_{Lg}
                  \\\
                  \\cdot  &amp; \stackrel{E(u,v)}{\to} &amp; \cdot
                  \\\ \downarrow^{Rf} &amp;&amp; \downarrow_{Rg} \\
                  \\cdot &amp;\stackrel{v}{\to}&amp; \cdot
                  }
          %p
            There are two other injective functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d^0, d^2 : 2 \rightarrow 3
            whose image misses the object that appears as their superscript. When we compose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_2
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^{2}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            There are obvious natural transformations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \Rightarrow R
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \Rightarrow 1
            whose components are given by the data of the functorial factorization
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            are
            %strong pointed
            endofunctors, with these natural transformations in mind.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A AWFS on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            consists of a pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,R)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is a comonad and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a monad, whose underlying pointed endofunctors arise from a functorial factorization
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi R
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi R
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L R \Rightarrow R L
          %p
            We refer to the
            = succeed "-coalgebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %strong left class
            of the AWFS and the
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "." do
              %strong right class
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %strong underlying WFS
            of this AWFS.
          %p Given a lifting problem
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi u
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi v
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\cdot &amp;\stackrel{u}{\to}&amp; \cdot
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^f
                  \&amp;&amp;
                  \\downarrow^g
                  \\\
                  \\cdot &amp;\stackrel{v}{\to}&amp; \cdot
                  }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a
            = succeed "-coalgebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            is an
            = succeed "-algebra," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %h2#interesting_features Interesting features
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The right class of a AWFS is closed under any limits that exist in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi K
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^{2}
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "-algebras" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %li
              %p Algebras for the monad of an AWFS can be composed canonically, as can the coalgebras for the comonad. The composition law for the algebras uses the comultiplication natural transformation, and dually for the coalgebras.
            %li
              %p
                Each AWFS
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L, R)
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                induces a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right-connected+double+category"} right-connected double category
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔸
                        %mi lg
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}lg
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Alg
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Alg
            %li
              %p
                A AWFS
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi L
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (L,R)
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                induces a levelwise AWFS on any diagram category
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi K
                          %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^A
            %li
              %p
                An AWFS can be detected as a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+factorization"} functorial factorization
                that extends to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi cod
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cod
                with a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition+law+for+factorizations"} composition law for factorizations
          %h2#small_object_argument Small object argument
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+small+object+argument"} algebraic small object argument
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
            due to Richard Garner, produces
            %strong cofibrantly generated
            AWFS by adapting the construction of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+monad"} free monad
            on an endofunctor. Importantly, Garner’s small object argument allows the generators to be a small category over the arrow category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi K
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K^{[2]}
          %p
            Every AWFS on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
            generated by such a small category of maps is in particular an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+weak+factorization+system"} accessible weak factorization system
            %em itself
            generated by the algebraic small object argument, unless we enhance it further to take a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            of generators; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BourkeGarnerI"} Bourke and Garner
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+model+category"} algebraic model category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+model+category"} accessible model category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+weak+factorization+system"} accessible weak factorization system
          %div
            %p
              %strong Algebraic model structures:
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+structures"} Quillen model structures
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+with+weak+equivalences"} categories with weak equivalences
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+systems"} weak factorization systems
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th structure
                  %th small-set-generated
                  %th small-category-generated
                  %th algebraicized
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+wfs"} combinatorial wfs
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+wfs"} accessible wfs
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+wfs"} algebraic wfs
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model category
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+model+category"} accessible model category
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+model+category"} algebraic model category
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfinite+construction+of+free+algebras"} construction method
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+object+argument"} small object argument
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    same as
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo →
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+small+object+argument"} algebraic small object argument
          %h2#references References
          %p A precursor to the notion appears in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fred+Linton"}> Fred Linton
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Coequalizers in categories of algebras
          %p The modern notion, missing the distributivity axiom, was introduced in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"} Marco Grandis
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Tholen"} Walter Tholen
              = succeed "," do
                %em Natural weak factorization systems
          %p The missing axiom was introduced in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"}> Richard Garner
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Understanding the small object argument
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0712.0724"} arXiv
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic model structures
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0910.2733"} arXiv:0910.2733
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Athorne"}> Thomas Athorne
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The coalgebraic structure of cell complexes
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/26/11/26-11abs.html"} TAC
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Barthel"} Tobias Barthel
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"} Emily Riehl
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the construction of functorial factorizations for model categories
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.5427"} arxiv
            %li#BourkeGarnerI
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Bourke"} John Bourke
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic weak factorisation systems I: Accessible AWFS
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6559"} arXiv:1412.6559
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpaa.2015.06.002"} doi:10.1016/j.jpaa.2015.06.002
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Bourke"} John Bourke
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+Garner"} Richard Garner
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic weak factorisation systems II: Categories of weak maps
                = succeed "," do
